## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key insight is that while Amy Coney Barrett did hold up a blank notepad during her confirmation hearing on October 13, 2020, creating a viral moment, no primary sources or transcripts contain the quoted statement that she was not using notes "because I have seven kids and I'm used to stupid searches." The hearing transcripts reviewed do not contain this quote, and the described event does not connect her lack of notes to her children or the specific phrase "stupid questions."

We are still lacking any direct evidence from a credible source—such as a transcript, video, or contemporaneous report—that Barrett made the specific statement attributed to her in the claim. The evidence only supports that the notepad event occurred, not the quoted reasoning.

Therefore, the knowledge gathered from the fact-check explicitly refutes the specific quoted claim, as no evidence supports that Barrett said those words during the hearing. `refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check refutes the claim because, while Amy Coney Barrett did hold up a blank notepad during her confirmation hearing on October 13, 2020, no primary sources—including the [full hearing transcript](https://www.rev.com/blog/transcripts/amy-coney-barrett-senate-confirmation-hearing-day-1-transcript)—contain the quoted statement that she was not using notes "because I have seven kids and I'm used to stupid questions." The viral moment, as reported by sources like the [New York Post](https://nypost.com/2020/10/13/amy-coney-barrett-reveals-she-has-no-notes-for-hearing/), involved her showing a blank notepad in a lighthearted exchange, but it was not linked to the specific reasoning or phrasing attributed in the claim.